From: DavideD-bot <DavideD-bot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 15:27:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix NORMALIZE timezone storage mode ignored for
 OffsetDateTime/ZonedDateTime on dialects with NATIVE timezone support

The guards in adaptTimestampTypesToDefaultTimeZoneStorage() and
adaptTimeTypesToDefaultTimeZoneStorage() used 'instanceof JavaTimeJdbcType'
to decide whether to skip overriding the type for NORMALIZE mode.
However, the baseline JdbcTypeBaseline always registers OffsetDateTimeJdbcType
and ZonedDateTimeJdbcType, both of which implement JavaTimeJdbcType, so the
guard always fired and the NORMALIZE override was never applied.

The intent of the guard is to avoid clobbering a type that has been configured
for direct Java time JDBC access (via hibernate.type.java_time_use_direct_jdbc).
Replace the instanceof check with a proper isDirectJavaTimeJdbcAccessEnabled()
call that only skips the override when direct access is actually enabled for
the specific type AND supported by the dialect.

This fixes PassThruZonedTest (NORMALIZE mode) on SQL Server, which has
TimeZoneSupport.NATIVE but does not enable direct Java time JDBC access
by default.
